# humans — the session-mail humans registry.
#
# One canonical local-part per line. A send with any of these on To, Cc or
# Bcc OWES A RENDERED HTML PART (markdown is the default body form; humans
# get both). Matching is exact: case-folded, +tag stripped, NO dash/dot
# normalisation — `alice.smith` and `alice` are different entries on purpose.
#
# ADDING A HUMAN IS EDITING THIS FILE — never a code change, never a
# reinstall. This shipped copy is the FINAL fallback; a deployment overrides
# it with $SESSIONMAIL_ROOT/.humans (beside the mail boxes — DOT-PREFIXED so
# no address can ever spell it and no mailbox can ever collide with it, RFC
# 5321 §4.1.2), and tests point $SESSIONMAIL_HUMANS at a
# temp file. (The old SESSION_MAIL_* spellings are read too, for one release.)
#
# This shipped copy names NOBODY (no person's name in code). When it
# is the list in use, the host's operator is a human too: `operator.mailbox`
# in ~/.sessionkit/tools/settings/session.json. A host with no operator configured
# has no human here until it adds one, above or in its own .humans.
